37 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446237 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446237 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da37f824-4e73-5b4a-a18f-250ab74de323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446238 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446238 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446238 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446238 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446238 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186446214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446238 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446238 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446238 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446238 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e6dc0783-a199-5ca8-ace2-ec2c865c730a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446239 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446239 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446239 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446239 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $g(x,y)=xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨3,-4⟩?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446239 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30b363a1-05a8-5997-8feb-6bd8aaa30fa0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446239 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{x+y}+xy{e}^{x+y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{x+y}+xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y(1+x){e}^{x+y}+2xy,\\,x(1+y){e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,-1) & =⟨(-1)\\cdot (1+1){e}^{1-1}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1),\\,1\\cdot (1-1){e}^{1-1}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1) & =∇\\,g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446240 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446240 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446240 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446240 #edb/ref 1071 "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446240 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3642d7fd-0113-5cbf-a61e-17061219f8d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446240 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇g(x,y,z) & =-⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z))⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x+z},\\,2xy,\\,-\\frac{4}{x+z}⟩ \\\\ -∇g(1,1,1) & =-⟨{1}^{2}-\\frac{4}{1+1},\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{4}{1+1}⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446241 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the function  $g(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)^{2}$, find its directional derivative at the\npoint $A(-1,1)$ in the direction that's towards the point $B(3,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c3374df-275b-5fb9-8864-40efeec8b5b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446241 #edb/ref 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,4⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4x({x}^{2}+y),\\,2({x}^{2}+y)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1),2\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446242 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446242 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446242 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446242 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ at the point $A(4,3)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446242 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fc2a3e0-1de4-50ca-b644-c592b3edb577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446242 #edb/ref 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,1⟩-⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}}⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}⟨-3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(4,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(4,3) & =⟨\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(4,3) & =∇f(4,3)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4(-3)+3(-2)}{5\\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5\\sqrt{13}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446243 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446243 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130811" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446243 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446243 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{xy}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446243 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72e3df1a-6338-564c-adc2-85283b26953d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446243 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(8,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\,j \\\\ -∇f(8,2) & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{8}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction of $-∇f(8,2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇f(8,2)‖ & =‖-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446244 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446244 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446244 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨5,12⟩?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446244 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "754f0abc-16b3-51ff-9446-aa81fd4c05d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446244 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(2,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}}=13 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{13}⟨5,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x,\\,-6y⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,1) & =⟨8(2),-6(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨16,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(2,1) & =∇g(2,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨16,-6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}⟨5,12⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{16(5)-6(12)}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{80-72}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446245 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446245 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446245 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446245 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $g(x,y)={x}^{y}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446245 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d39de67-7fd2-5aeb-a063-e9cf738816af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446245 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{x}^{y-1},\\,{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨(1){1}^{1-1},\\,({1}^{1})\\operatorname{ln}⁡1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $∇g(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(1,1)‖ & =‖⟨1,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446246 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446246 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446246 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446246 #edb/ref 1071 "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446246 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "beaf6ecd-5ac6-5efb-ad1b-41d64d5bbd40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446246 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6x,\\,-2y,\\,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1) & =⟨-6(1),-2(-\\frac{1}{2}),-3(-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨-6,1,-3⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446247 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446247 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86539" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446247 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446247 #edb/ref 1071 "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,2,-1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446247 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9974a87-21df-58c9-86ba-41826947b876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446247 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{xy},\\,x{e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2},\\,6yz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,2,-1) & =⟨2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2},\\,2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2}+3(-1)^{2},\\,6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
